00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.700
hey everybody, for today's problem we have a
reaction here that I've drawn, this is

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:16.309
this compound reacting with sulfuric
acid to generate this product, and what

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:18.920
we're gonna do is we want to show the
mechanism, that means every single

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.520
intermediate and every single electron
pushing arrow, so other than the fact that

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.660
we're reacting with with a strong acid i don't
want to give too much away

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.120
go ahead and give it a shot

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.880
ok so this is a pretty interesting
mechanism let's take a look at what

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.440
happens, now we know that this is a
strong acid, it has the capacity to

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.220
protonate something. the only thing we're
gonna be able to protonate is this hydroxyl

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.040
group so anytime you see an acid-base
reaction, we've got a strong acid, we know

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.840
that strong acid is gonna donate a proton so
we want to look at the substrate and say

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.100
where's the proton gonna go? well once
again the only thing here that's gonna

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.969
work, we have a lone pair on oxygen,
that's the only thing that can

00:01:06.969 --> 00:01:11.069
accommodate a proton, so that is gonna
get protonated and now we have basically

00:01:11.069 --> 00:01:15.590
a water molecule right here. now whereas
a hydroxyl group is a is a terrible

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:19.229
leaving group, a water molecule is a
great leaving group and we are in acidic

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:26.319
condition so positive charges are ok, so
this water can leave so that if

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:30.289
water leaves, now we're left with a carbocation right here. now this is the

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:34.959
key step, this is a carbocation
rearrangement step. if we remember from

00:01:34.959 --> 00:01:38.579
the other lectures we can have a hydride
shift or we can have a methanide

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:42.929
shift where actual alkyl groups are
moving, but the interesting thing here is

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:48.119
that this is gonna be able to rearrange
to turn a secondary carbocation into a

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:51.599
tertiary carbocation and the way
it's going to be able to do it is with

00:01:51.599 --> 00:01:56.979
this methanide shift right there. or
it's not really a methyl but an alkyl

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:02.659
shift where this bond is going to
detach from that carbon and reattach

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:07.020
to that carbon so this is what we're
gonna get. this is favorable for two

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:07.810
reasons

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.550
number one: we turn a secondary carbocation

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:14.710
into a tertiary carbocation so that's a driving force

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.690
but then the other thing we're doing is
turning a five-membered ring into a

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:21.650
six-membered ring, so there's a lot going
on here that makes this a favorable

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.190
rearrangement. and so now from the
secondary carbocation we have this

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:30.440
tertiary carbocation, and we are in aqueous acidic
solution so we can always eliminate and

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:36.930
what's going to happen is this can grab
this proton here and form the pi bond

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.930
there, and there's our product.

